MultirationalMap -- the class of all multi-rational maps
********************************************************

Description
===========

A multi-rational map is a rational map between *note multi-projective
varieties: MultiprojectiveVariety,, $$\Phi:X\subseteq
\mathbb{P}^{r_1}\times\mathbb{P}^{r_2}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{P}^{r_n}\
dashrightarrow Y \subseteq
\mathbb{P}^{s_1}\times\mathbb{P}^{s_2}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{P}^{s_m}
.$$Thus, it can be represented by an *note ordered list: (Macaulay2Doc)List, of
*note rational maps: (Cremona)RationalMap,$$\Phi_i = (\Phi:X\dashrightarrow
Y)\circ(pr_i:Y\to Y_i\subseteq\mathbb{P}^{s_i}) ,$$for $i=1,\ldots,m$. The maps
$\Phi_i:X\dashrightarrow Y_i\subseteq\mathbb{P}^{s_i}$, since the target $Y_i$
is a standard projective variety, are implemented with the class *note
RationalMap: (Cremona)RationalMap, (more properly, when $n>1$ the class of such
maps is called MultihomogeneousRationalMap). Recall that the main constructor
for the class *note RationalMap: (Cremona)RationalMap, (as well as for the
class MultihomogeneousRationalMap) is the method *note rationalMap:
(Cremona)rationalMap,.

The constructor for the class of multi-rational maps is *note multirationalMap:
multirationalMap,, which can often be abbreviated to *note rationalMap:
rationalMap_lpList_cmMultiprojectiveVariety_rp, (see also *note shortcuts:
shortcuts,). It takes as input the list of maps $\{\Phi_1:X\dashrightarrow
Y_1,\ldots,\Phi_m:X\dashrightarrow Y_m\}$, together with the variety $Y$, and
returns the map $\Phi:X\dashrightarrow Y$.

* Menu:

* multirationalMap::            the multi-rational map defined by a list of rational maps
* rationalMap(List,MultiprojectiveVariety): rationalMap_lpList_cmMultiprojectiveVariety_rp.  the multi-rational map defined by a list of rational maps

