GG -- the Grassmannian of k-dimensional linear subspaces of an n-dimensional projective space
*********************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        GG(k,PP_K^n)
        GG_K(k,n)
        GG Grass(k,n,K,Variable=>"x")
  * Inputs:
      * k, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,
      * P, an *note embedded projective variety: EmbeddedProjectiveVariety,, a
        projective space of dimension $n$
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Grassmannian variety: GrassmannianVariety,, which parametrizes
        the $k$-dimensional subspaces of $\mathbb{P}^n$

Description
===========

+---------------------------------+
|i1 : GG(2,PP_QQ^5)               |
|                                 |
|o1 = GG(2,5) ⊂ PP^19             |
|                                 |
|o1 : ProjectiveVariety, GG(2,5)  |
+---------------------------------+
|i2 : describe oo                 |
|                                 |
|o2 = ambient:.............. PP^19|
|     dim:.................. 9    |
|     codim:................ 10   |
|     degree:............... 42   |
|     generators:........... 2^35 |
|     purity:............... true |
|     dim sing. l.:......... -1   |
+---------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note GG(ZZ,MultirationalMap): GG_lpZZ_cmMultirationalMap_rp, -- induced
    automorphism of the Grassmannian

Ways to use GG:
===============

  * "GG(EmbeddedProjectiveVariety)"
  * "GG(Ring)"
  * "GG(Ring,ZZ,ZZ)"
  * "GG(ZZ,EmbeddedProjectiveVariety)"
  * "GG(ZZ,ZZ)"
  * *note GG(ZZ,MultirationalMap): GG_lpZZ_cmMultirationalMap_rp, -- induced
    automorphism of the Grassmannian
  * "GG(ZZ,RationalMap)" -- see *note GG(ZZ,MultirationalMap):
    GG_lpZZ_cmMultirationalMap_rp, -- induced automorphism of the Grassmannian

For the programmer
==================

The object *note GG: GG, is a *note method function:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

